  "id": "badkhn_wedding_recitation",
  "name": "Badkhones (Yiddish wedding-jester recitation)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "The art of the badkhn, the Eastern European Ashkenazi wedding entertainer who improvised rhymed Yiddish verses at each stage of a traditional wedding, from the moralizing kale bazetsn that brought the bride to tears before the veiling to the jests and gift announcements at the feast, chanted over the sustained accompaniment of the klezmer band. Nineteenth-century badkhonim such as Elyokum Zunser of Vilna published their verses as songs, early Yiddish 78s preserved wedding routines, and the role continues at Hasidic weddings.",
  "recipe": "classic Eastern European mid-1930s badkhones, wedding, European branch, stage performance spoken word. recitative rhythmically free pitched speech, solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. ornamented fiddle, clarinet. klezmer simcha celebration hall. large-diaphragm condenser, tube pre, German tube console. 78rpm shellac.",
